300 ms + linear/no easing and minimum total index-shift as the tie-break are contractual requirements, I would add dedicated tests for both.
[Robolectric Test] MOVE duration is exactly 300 ms and uses linear progression
Freeze the Compose test clock and start a transition with a survivor that has a meaningful horizontal displacement. Record its source X as oldX and destination X as newX.
During the MOVE phase, check positions at known elapsed times:
MOVE start:    0 ms   → oldX
25%:          75 ms   → oldX + 0.25 × (newX - oldX)
50%:         150 ms   → oldX + 0.50 × (newX - oldX)
75%:         225 ms   → oldX + 0.75 × (newX - oldX)
MOVE end:     300 ms   → newX
The 25% and 75% assertions are useful. Testing only the midpoint is weaker because some easing curves can also pass through 50% position at 50% time.
This test establishes both:
translation lasts 300 ms;
translation is linear, with no easing.
I'd complement it with a Roborazzi screenshot at 75 ms or 225 ms if you want a visual regression test, but the deterministic position assertion is the stronger test.
[Robolectric/Unit Test] Minimum total index-shift is used after maximizing survivors
This needs a deliberately ambiguous string pair where there are multiple order-preserving matchings with the same maximum survivor count, but different total costs:
total cost = Σ |oldIndex - newIndex|
The test should verify that the matching with the smaller total cost wins—not a greedy locally-nearest match.
The priority being tested is:
1. Matches must preserve relative order
2. Maximize number of survivors
3. Among maximum-survivor solutions,
   minimize Σ |oldIndex - newIndex|
4. If still tied, resolve deterministically
This should preferably be a pure unit test against the internal matcher/planner, because screenshots make it unnecessarily difficult to determine precisely which duplicate occurrence was selected.



Here are the hard cases I'd keep in the hidden suite:
[Robolectric Test] Crossing would preserve more characters — ab → ba
A naive “maximize matching characters” implementation preserves both and crosses them. Your contract requires order-preserving survivors, so only one may survive. This catches greedy/global-character matching.
[Robolectric Test] Maximum survivors beat cheaper shuffle
Construct a case where preserving fewer characters produces a lower total index cost. The implementation must still choose the solution with more survivors. This catches algorithms that optimize shuffle cost before match count.
[Robolectric Test] Duplicate-heavy ambiguous matching — aabaa → ababa
There are several plausible ways to associate the repeated as and bs. Require maximum reuse, non-crossing order, minimum total index-shuffle cost, and deterministic tie-breaking. This is much harder than aaaa → aa.
[Robolectric Test] Adversarial duplicate matching — ababaa → baaaba
Use a less visually obvious repeated-character case. This is good for catching greedy “take nearest matching character” algorithms that work on your examples but don't find the globally optimal matching.
[Robolectric Test] Reverse duplicate patterns — aabbcc → ccbbaa
There are many equal characters but order preservation severely restricts what can survive. This catches implementations that independently match each character occurrence without enforcing monotonic destination indices.
[Robolectric Test] Return to source during active transition — A → B → A
Start A → B, then request A while that transition is still active. Expected committed progression is:
A → B → A
This is surprisingly easy to get wrong if code ignores a request because it equals the currently committed text when the request arrives.
[Robolectric Test] Conflation with return-to-source — A → B, pending C → D → A
While A → B is active, request C, then D, then A. The current transition must finish at B; all intermediate pending values are discarded; then it must perform B → A. This tests queuing and conflation together.
[Robolectric Test] Destination changes exactly at the POST-MOVE boundary
Start chair → care and issue hair on the frame where MOVE has finished but INSERT/commit has not happened. The implementation must not lose the update, interrupt the current commit, or transition from the wrong source. Expected committed progression remains:
chair → care → hair
[Robolectric Test] Destination changes on REMOVE frame
Start chair → care; while the observable REMOVE frame exists, request hair. The current chair → care transition must still complete before care → hair begins. This catches implementations whose LaunchedEffect(text) cancels the running animation whenever text changes.
[Robolectric Test] Update storm
While one transition is active, send perhaps 50–100 target changes without advancing enough time for the current transition to finish. Then advance the clock. Verify the current transition completes and the component ultimately reaches only the latest pending target. This catches accidental queues instead of conflation.
[Robolectric Test] Same destination repeatedly during transition
A → B, followed by B, B, B, B. It should finish at B and stop. No redundant transition should start. This catches poorly managed conflated requests.
[Robolectric Test] Alternating values rapidly — A/B/A/B/A/B...
Send alternating targets while the first transition is running. What matters is the value of the latest pending request when the active transition completes. This stresses assumptions based on text != committedText.
[Roborazzi Screenshot Test] Destination-underlay trap
chair → care, captured halfway through MOVE. This should be a strict golden. There must be exactly one copy of each survivor, and no e. A shortcut implementation such as drawing Text("care") underneath moving old characters will fail visibly.
[Roborazzi Screenshot Test] Rightward movement trap — care → hair
At REMOVE, a and r remain at old coordinates. During MOVE, r moves right while a remains where appropriate. This catches implementations that simply delete characters and animate the remaining string's normal relayout/left-compaction.
[Roborazzi Screenshot Test] Mixed-direction survivor movement
Find a transition where different survivors have different displacement behavior. This is stronger than a case where every survivor shifts in the same direction. It catches implementations that translate the whole surviving substring as one object instead of animating individual survivors.
[Roborazzi Screenshot Test] Proportional-font trap
Use wide/narrow glyphs with repeated characters, for example a transition involving W, i, and m. The matching still uses indices, but translation must use actual rendered X positions. This catches implementations that correctly solve matching but calculate x = index * characterWidth.
[Roborazzi Screenshot Test] Ligature-sensitive text
If your supported typography contract includes it, strings involving combinations such as fi can expose the difference between full-string shaping and independently rendered glyphs. If the assignment only promises simple LTR labels, I would not make this a required hidden test.
[Robolectric Test] Multi-code-unit grapheme matching
Use a combining sequence or emoji alongside ordinary letters. The test should catch implementations based on Kotlin Char, which can split a user-perceived character into multiple animation units.
[Robolectric Test] Grapheme duplicates
Even harder: repeat a multi-code-unit grapheme and transition between different counts/positions. This combines Unicode segmentation with duplicate matching.
[Robolectric Test] Recomposition during MOVE
Trigger unrelated recompositions every few frames while MOVE is running. The transition must continue rather than restart from progress zero. This catches animation state accidentally keyed to composition state.
[Robolectric Test] Style changes during queued transitions
Start A → B, queue C, then change TextStyle while A→B is active. It should neither lose C nor restart the active transition. This stresses measurement, state ownership, and coroutine lifetime simultaneously.
[Robolectric Test] Dispose exactly during MOVE
Remove the component from composition halfway through an animation. There should be no leaked job or exception. Recreate it afterward with a different value and ensure no old transition state leaks into the new instance.
[Robolectric Test] Two instances receiving rapid independent updates
Animate two MorphingTexts simultaneously with different update storms. This catches accidentally shared/global animation or matching state.
[Robolectric Test] Frame-boundary correctness with zero movement
Pick a transition where surviving characters already have their destination positions. Even though MOVE has no visible displacement, REMOVE and POST-MOVE/INSERT ordering must remain correct. An implementation shouldn't collapse phases just because translation distance happens to be zero.
[Robolectric + Roborazzi Test] Removed everything, then insert everything
abc → XYZ has zero survivors. The hard part isn't matching; it's state-machine correctness. There must still be a state with no old or new characters before the destination appears. Some implementations special-case “no matches” and immediately show the destination.
The strongest hidden-test combination
If you want the assessment to stay manageable but still expose weak implementations, I'd prioritize these eight:
ab → ba
    non-crossing matching

aabaa → ababa
    globally optimal duplicate matching

A → B → A while A→B is active
    return-to-source bug

A → B with C → D → A queued
    conflation + return-to-source

update exactly at POST-MOVE
    race/frame-boundary handling

care → hair midpoint golden
    true per-survivor translation

chair → care midpoint golden
    no destination underlay / duplicate copies

abc → XYZ
    zero-survivor phase ordering
